<P>&#9;Raising my voice and peering around again, I called back to the elf.  His archer friend had disappeared.  "Who calls me out?"</P><br><P>&#9;"I am Wilitin Windwalker of Kelthin and scout-leader of the of the Emerald Warriors!"</P><br><P>&#9;"Then if you have a god, Windwalker, pray to him, and I'll give my love to your family!  You are already dead!"  &#9;</P><br><P>&#9;In one fluid motion I turned and whirled into the doorway, drawing the fletch to my cheek.  The brazen bastard was just standing there...no cover in sight.</P><br><P>&#9;My bow sang as the black arrow sped to its target.  It hit him straight in the chest and the force of it crushed the air out of him and threw him onto his back.  Somehow, though...it did not stick, but fell away, as though it had struck a stone.  Still, the force of it had knocked the breath out of him and I heard his curse as I nocked the second arrow.</P><br><P>I looked at Kheldan, now standing at the other side of the doorway, "I hit him dead on...and his hide turned it!"</P><br><P>Kheldan's brow furrowed, "Hmm...well shoot 'em agian...perhaps you missed."</P><br><P>&#9;I knew I had not missed, but there was no time to argue it.  Again I whirled to shoot....and stopped, taking in the new situation in an instant, for that was all the time I had.</P><br><P>&#9;The human girl behind him had unlslung a mace from her belt.  She had apparently just helped Windwalker to his feet and stood again behind him.  As he rose, the steel of the mace slammed down onto the top of his head so hard I could hear it from where I stood.  His legs wobbled and he sunk to his knees.  Again the mace fell and he rolled limp to the ground.</P><br><P>&#9;I ducked back inside...the archer was still out there somewhere.  "Hmm....that's odd."</P><br><P>&#9;"What is?"  Asked Khel.</P><br><P>&#9;"I think he and his girlfriend are having a spat."</P><br><P>&#9;Again I heard the mace strike him.</P><br><P>&#9;"Or more of a tiff, maybe." I added.</P><br><P>&#9;"They're turnin on each other then?"</P><br><P>&#9;"Seems that way, yes."</P><br><P>&#9;"Good.  One less to worry about."</P><br><P>&#9;"Right.  Ok...two left.  We'll rush them.  You take the girl. I'll get the archer.&quot;  I readied myself to dash out the door.</P><br><P>&#9;Khel scowled, "The girl?"</P><br><P>&#9;"Yes, and I'll get the archer."</P><br><P>&#9;"Uhmm..."</P><br><P>&#9;"What?"</P><br><P>&#9;"Is na manly to hit a girl..." he said.</P><br><P>&#9;"uh….what?"</P><br><P>&#9;"Is not polite..."</P><br><P>&#9;"Khel, you are a <B><I>Barbarian</B></I>!  When did it become important to be <B><I>polite</B></I>!?  She's armed and she is here to kill us....</P><br><P>&#9;"You, ya mean."</P><br><P>"Right, very well, she is here to kill <B>me</B>.  See here, you don’t have to kill her, just hit her on the head with your stick.  Occupy her while I get that archer!"</P><br><P>&#9;"Hmm...alright...but I'm na gonna hit her hard."</P><br><P>&#9;&#9;</P><br><P>&#9;I will never understand Barbarians.  The most brutal warriors in Norrath--short of ogres and trolls, perhaps--and they have a problem hitting a lady.</P><br><br><P>&#9;To me a lady gives up the privilege of being demure, when she draws a weapon.</P><br><br><P>&#9;Our reverie was broken by another arrow slamming into the doorframe.  It was another of the long, delicate piercers as we had seen back at the stream.</P><br><P>&#9;Turning again around the doorway I pulled again at my bow--and was surprised to see the archer, standing, as had Windwalker, in plain sight on the hillside, nocking another arrow to the string.  The girl was nowhere to be seen.</P><br><P>&#9;If this was the way the archers of the Emerald Warriors all hunted, I could not see how they had survived as a race for so long.  My arrow sang to its target and hit him square in the stomach.</P><br><P>&#9;And bounced off.</P><br><P>&#9;I blinked a few times before the hiss of his arrow started me back behind the wall.  What in the name of the Mother of All!?</P><br><P>&#9;I thought only for a moment.</P><br><P>&#9;Of course.</P><br><P>&#9;Quickly, I reached up and yanked his arrow free of the doorframe, and another of his shafts cut my hand as  pulled it back.</P><br><P> &#9;Once more I turned to fire.  Still he stood without cover, advancing down the hill as he nocked another arrow.  He was perhaps a hundred feet away--close enough for me to see his confident smile.</P><br><P>&#9;I fired.</P><br><P>&#9;And the smile faded slowly from his face, as the piercer arrow passed cleanly through his body, as though he were not even there. </P><br><P>&#9;He toppled to the ground dead.</P><br><P>&#9;Turning quickly, my eyes scanned the twilight of the hillside for the last enemy—the girl.  But I had no time to see her.  </P><br><P> My strength….</P><br><P>The hate that had welled in me was gone, and with it went my strength.  My leg seemed on fire, and yet numb at the same time, and my head was light.  I could not feel the ground as I slowly lay upon it.</P><br><P>It was twilight…and yet somehow it was darker…</P><br><P>&quot;Khel…&quot;  I whispered, &quot;Khel, I’m cold…&quot;</P><br><br><P>Darkness enveloped me and as I relaxed into the warm embrace of the stones and the earth…there was her face…</P><br><br><P>Layla…</P><br><P></P><br><CENTER>***</CENTER>         <br>
